    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Far from simply mediating sexual development and reproduction, steroid sex hormones play a large role in the regulation of complex social behaviors across species. Several studies have elucidated how sex steroid hormones (i.e., androgens and estrogens) can increase adult male sexual and aggressive behaviors. But just how sex steroid hormones influence the onset of social behavior in both sexes during early ontogeny remains to be established. Here, we use the African cichlid fish Astatotilapia burtoni, to investigate how early sex hormone exposure influences the transition from gregarious shoaling behavior to aggressive chasing behavior in juveniles. Three equally sized groups of eggs from the same brood were separated and immersed into aquarium water containing a known concentration of androgen, estrogen, or control (dimethyl sulfoxide or DMSO) shortly after fertilization. At three time points up to two months after fertilization, the movements of six fish from each treatment group were video recorded to map their individual positions over time. We found that fish in the androgen-treated group shoaled (measured by the mean similarity between trajectories) significantly less than those in the estrogen- and DMSO-treated groups at the latter two time points, suggesting that early androgen exposure has an organizational effect in the brain that facilitates a premature transition to aggressive behavior from shoaling behavior. As all vertebrates—including humans—share many of the same sex hormones and associated receptors with A. burtoni, these results might inform our understanding of how differential prenatal sex hormone exposure could influence social behavior later in life.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
